INTERNATIONAL EDUCATION STATISTICS AND RESEARCH:
STATUS AND PROBLEMS*
JEFFREY M. PURYEAR
The Inter-AmericanDialogue, 211 Connecticut Ave., N.W., Washington, DC 20036, U.S.A.
Abstract -- Although governments throughout the world invest heavily in education, they
spend remarkably little to monitor and evaluate their investment. Management of most of the
world's education systemsis done without adequate informationand analysis. The international
education database is often unreliable, and leaves out crucial measures of quality, process and
output. Education research does not receive the same priority as research on agriculture, health or the economy. These shortcomings are due in part to the limited professional capacity most countries have to gather education statistics and carry out education research, along with strong resistance by educators and policymakersto being evaluated. Improvements will depend heavily on greater commitment at the political level to measuring education outputs,
and on the emergence of global leadership in promoting comprehensive, reliable assessments of education systems.
I. INTRODUCTION
Governments throughout the world are underinvesting in educational statistics and research. Most spend between 10 and 25 per cent of their budgets on education -- an amount equal to over 5 per cent of world GNP (UNESCO, 1993, 104). They expect those investments to promote economic growth, social equity and national integration. Yet little of that money is used to analyze educational systems. Government statistics on education are uniformly narrow, seldom timely and often unreliable. Most countries lack systematic measures of educational output. Cross-national educational assessments are rare. Few governments allocate significant resources to educational research. As a result, the world has only limited information with which to monitor and evaluate one of its major investments. lust as remarkably, deficiencies in the world's capacity to monitor and evaluate educational systems have attracted little attention. There is no vigorous, global debate over the reliability of educational statistics, nor over what is learned
