II. PRINCIPAL PROBLEMS
Existing statistics are unreliable
The existing international education database is far too often unreliable. To be sure, data on education are better overall than they were in the 1940s and 1950s. Then, approximately 50 countries regularly reported information on their education systems, and much of that was not readily comparable cross-nationally. Today some 175 countries regularly provide at least minimal information within the framework of the International Standard Classification of Education (ISCED). Data collected by the advanced industrial countries have expanded over the past several decades, and often exceed that requested by UNESCO. Several develop
