ing countries have significantly improved the quality of their education statistics. But quality, even where improving, is still too low overall. Many of the world's poorest countries have made little progress in producing reliable data on their educational systems. Some have moved backward, and today do a poorer job of collecting data than they did during the 1960s and 1970s. Some twenty to thirty countries suffer disastrous problems in generating reliable education statistics. Another fifty or so report data that have significant gaps and weaknesses. Overall, the problem is with the quality of the data reported rather than with a failure to report. Only a few countries report little or no data. Typically such lapses occur when broader political strife renders impossible the normal functions of government. Occasionally they occur in countries that, although not experiencing political turmoil, possess extraordinarily inept state bureaucracies. More commonly, however, countries report data based on collection procedures that are fundamentally flawed, or on definitions that are either non-standard or poorly understood. It is on the ground, where raw statistics are gathered and processed, that the reliability of existing data is most commonly compromised (Chapman and Boothroyd, 1988). The principal problems afflicting global education statistics are at the national, rather than the international, level. Examples, unfortunately, are easy to find. Education statistics from at least five of the world's nine largest countries (Nigeria, Brazil, Pakistan, Egypt and Bangladesh) are judged by most experts to have serious deficiencies (UNESCO staff, personal interviews, 12-13 November 1992). A number of the smaller Caribbean countries report only limited information on their educational systems (UNESCO, 1991a,c). Brazilian education statistics reported in 1982 that 5.1 million new students had enrolled in the first year of primary school, despite the fact that only 3 million children were in the relevant age cohort (Costa Ribeiro, 1990). In Uganda, the Ministries of Education and Finance cannot agree on how many primary teachers the country has -- one (Finance) counts some 85,000 and the other (Education) some 140,000 (World Bank staff member, telephone conversation, 19 November 1992). Many countries, as a UNESCO official recently noted, "do not know what they spend on
education." In Syria recently some 50 per cent more students graduated from high school than had been projected, causing serious problems for the country's higher education system. In the mid-1980s, Somalia reported nearly twice as many teachers as were later determined to be present in schools. Generally, the poorest, worst-educated countries also have the least reliable education statistics. But countries as diverse as the Congo, Jordan and Argentina consistently report unreliable information on their systems of education (UNESCO staff, personal interviews, 12-13 November 1992). Problems are particularly severe in subSaharan Africa, where several decades of political turmoil and economic decline have seriously eroded an already weak statistical base. In many of those countries, the minimum set of trained people, institutions, funds, incentives and political will necessary to collect accurate education statistics is not in place. Specialists speak of the "collapse of the most basic information on education" in some of them (International Institute for Educational Planning staff member, personal interview, 13 November 1992). A process of state deterioration and deinstitutionalization is underway in Africa that profoundly undercuts the ability of those countries to collect and report reliable education statistics. The countries of Central and Eastern Europe, and the former Soviet Union, also present a special challenge. For many years their education statistics were unreliable because of political pressures to demonstrate the success of Socialism. Now most of the old channels have been abolished, and new channels for collecting and reporting information are being set up. But the new systems are being developed within the context of serious economic hardship, and are not yet functioning well. Whether they will manage to produce reliable education statistics remains to be seen. Worldwide, some of the most consistently unreliable data are on literacy. UNESCO's 1958 definition of an illiterate - - someone "who cannot with understanding both read and write a short simple statement on his everyday life" - - has been operationalized in different ways in different countries (UNESCO, 1990b). Many governments draw their literacy data from the census, based on asking respondents whether they can read and write. Some simply equate literacy with the proportion of the population


