The statistical regime is too narrow
A second problem is that the information going into the international database on education is too narrow. The statistics that countries regularly gather, even when reliable, fail to cover some of the most fundamental aspects of education. Most resources have been dedicated to a single approach to monitoring educational systems: counting the inputs. Governments have concentrated almost entirely on recording the number of teachers, pupils and buildings in the formal system, and the size of public educational expenditures. They have given virtually no attention to documenting how schools function or what students learn. Therefore much of the data that could help determine where scarce educational resources ought to be allocated, or how effectively they are being used, are simply not on hand. The narrowness inherent in this approach is both methodological and conceptual. Methodologically, governments have been dominated by what specialists often call a "headcounting" mentality that has led them to monitor educational progress through what is essentially a census. UNESCO asks them to supply a "full count" of pupils, teachers, classrooms and budgetary expenditures (UNESCO, 1992a). They attempt to establish a systemwide capacity to generate that count annually. Relying almost exclusively on census-type head counts, however, has serious drawbacks. Head counts are expensive. They require stable institutions, considerable expertise and the ability to enjoin the entire educational system regularly to produce uniform statistics - resources that many countries lack. A majority of governments, for example, fail to report the age data that UNESCO requests, making it impossible to calculate net enrollment ratios and admission rates. In-kind expenditures, such as help in building schools - - often important in developing countries -- are almost never reported. Only about half the OECD countries can report private expenditures on education by level (OECD, 1992). Most countries report nothing at all on private education expenditures, and UNESCO's Statistical Yearbook leaves them out completely. That omission is important. For example, there exist virtually no systematic data on the private costs of the "dual" system of vocational training that is in place in Germany, Austria and Switzerland, even though many
argue that it is a model for other countries. The existing education statistics database is particularly ill-suited to advising countries how much they are investing in education and training. Moreover, the current system of educational censuses fails to count many important inputs. Systematic global data on the training possessed by teachers at the various levels of education are unavailable. Information on the family backgrounds of students -- key to identifying and addressing the special problems of migrants - - is not generally collected. Nor is information collected on the availabilityof texts and other educational materials in the classroom. Many statistics cannot be disaggregated into such simple but important geographical classifications as rural and urban. Major categories of education, such as nonformal and adult education, carried on outside the formal system are not included in the official statistics reported to UNESCO. Apprenticeships, short courses and part-time programs that occur outside the formal system are likely to be left out. Private educational facilities in general, including some religious schools, are not well covered. The information available on education worldwide refers almost exclusively to the formal education system. The counting mentality that is inherent in traditional approaches to national education statistics has, in addition, focused attention on the most elementary forms of information about education, and worked against the development and use of more sophisticated measures. Policymakers are presented with basic statistics rather than with carefully developed indicators - - for example, with enrollments rather than admission rates. Thus they do not learn the potential of such statistics, and may not allocate funds for them. The dominant approach, in the words of UNESCO specialists, is "collect and file" rather than "understand and use" (UNESCO staff member, personal interview, 12 November 1992). That emphasis does not encourage a more dynamic, instrumental approach to monitoring education systems among educators. It reinforces traditional tendencies to consider education in terms of stated policies rather than in terms of observed results. The government's analytic capacity, if present at all, is often located someplace other than the Ministry of Education. The traditional dominance of a counting mentality has worked against developing agree


