household surveys and elaborate indicators to complement their information on the economy, they might move beyond census-type counts and basic statistics to understand and assess their education systems. Yet sample surveys, indicators and modern social science research are conspicuously absent from the approaches most countries use to monitor their educational systems. Conceptually, governments have focused almost exclusively on educational inputs, to the detriment of process and output measures. Most of the fundamental data included in the international database on education - - classrooms, expenditures, teachers and pupils-- are about inputs. UNESCO asks governments to report annually how many students they enroll at each level, but not how much those students are taught or learn. It requests information on the number of classrooms a country has, but not on what takes place within them. We systematically collect imperfect data worldwide on the costs of education, but we collect no such data on the benefits. The existing regime of education statistics, in the words of one specialist, is "fascinated with inputs" (UNESCO staff member, personal interview, 13 November 1992). There are, for example, almost no measures of educational quality in the UNESCO database. We can approach the question of quality only by looking at statistics on repetition, dropouts and pupil-teacher ratios -all of them input-based measures, and at best uncertain proxies for educational quality. Nor do we have global information on educational processes, such as curriculum, language of instruction, teaching materials, number of hours spent in classrooms, or teaching methods. Similarly, there is almost no systematic, global information on educational output. UNESCO's statistics include only one direct output measure: adult illiteracy. We can approach output somewhat less directly by looking at the number of children completing each year or level of the educational system. But we lack data that are richer, more qualitative, and that address directly the question of what schools produce. Particularly conspicuous is the absence of educational achievement data. Most of the existing information lies outside the UNESCO system, and consists of national tests administered in perhaps 40 or 50 countries, and often


