not comparable cross-nationally. By far the best source of internationally comparable data on educational outcomes is the International Association for the Evaluation of Educational Achievement (IEA). Over 30 years, lEA has generated a variety of cross-national educational achievement studies that have become global benchmarks, both for their methodology and for their results (Hayes, 1992; Elley, 1992 and Husrn, 1967). But lEA studies so far have concentrated heavily on advanced industrial countries. They also are not periodic, and are driven more by the concerns of research than by a policy agenda. Thus although lEA studies may be helpful to those policymakers who read them, they do not provide the systematic, comprehensive view that policymakers need. More generally, the idea of regularly assessing the performance of educational systems through timely, empirical data has not yet taken root. Very few countries have their own systems for doing so, and no global system has begun to take shape. UNESCO, for example, has had limited involvement in achievement-testing over the years. As a recent UNICEF document noted: "In most countries today there is no objective measurement to monitor the real progress made in achieving basic education objectives, essential in light of the Jontiem conference" (UNICEF, 1990a). These problems often stand in sharp contrast to economic, demographic and health statistics, which tend generally to be more comprehensive and reliable than those available on education. Governments have focused their resources on just one, relatively narrow, approach to monitoring educational systems - - a censustype count - - and to a single type of data - inputs. That regime requires institutions and capacities that are beyond the reach of many countries, and leaves out many elements that are crucial to evaluating educational performance.
Research is not widely available, or widely used
A third problem is that many, if not most, countries fail to complement statistics with research in their efforts to monitor educational systems. To be sure, educational research has grown significantly worldwide over the past two decades. Researchers, institutions, publications and training programs have increased
dramatically. A series of subregional research networks have been established, and are becoming more active. Cross-national research is more common, and more sophisticated, than it was twenty years ago. But those advances are nonetheless limited in scope, and trail significantlybehind progress made in such fields as agriculture and health. The Consultative Group on International Agriculture Research (CGIAR), for example, raises more than $300 million dollars annually just for its 13 international agricultural research centers (CGIAR, 1992, 33). Those funds are complemented by over $8 billion dollars each year in public spending on agricultural research at the national level (Pardey et al., 1991). In health, approximately $35 billion dollars is spent each year on research worldwide (Commission on Health Research for Development, 1990). By contrast, reliable estimates on support for educational research worldwide are not even available, because no institution has been delegated and funded to gather them. Nothing similar to the CGIAR system exists for education, however, and there is no significant global initiative to promote research on education. 1 Educational research remains underfunded and underappreciated worldwide. Even in the United States, federal funding for educational research between 1980 and 1987 declined by one-third in real terms, while federal support for research in all areas grew by one-quarter (Wise, 1991).2 Many countries carry out no educational research at all. Most carry out significantly less research on education than they do on the economy, or on the public health system. Too much of what is carried out is weak. Governments worldwide have not made educational research a high priority. In the absence of good research, education often escapes the rigorous, empirical analysis that is applied more commonly to the economy or to the public health system. Countries tend to analyze their investments in education less, and less carefully, than they do many of their other investments. And even when they identify problems facing students and schools, they often fail to understand their causes. We know considerably more about costs, processes, output and demand associated with industrial production than we do about their counterparts in education. We have not studied the determinants of learning and the


