impact of teaching the way we have studied the prevention and treatment of disease. Education policy therefore is too often based on imprecise data, guessing or purely political considerations. It is too seldom informed by efforts to confirm, explain and evaluate educational outcomes. In consequence, policy declarations tend to become the center of attention rather than policy results. For much of the world, educational research has not yet become one of the tools policymakers use to g~aide their decisions.
Demand is growing more rapidly than supply Education statistics and research have not only lagged behind education's importance to society, but also behind society's interest in them. Although the effective demand for better educational assessment remains incipient, it has increased significantly over the past five years. The advance of democracy, new approaches to economic policy, greater attention to the performance and accountability of governments, and greater access to information are generating a new and more sophisticated understanding of education and ils uses. Whereas enrollments were a major concern throughout the 1970s, today the major emphasis is on quality. Increasingly, parents are concentrated about the quality of the education their children receive, rather than the quantity. Employers compelled to compete in an international economy take a sharper interest in the skills their employees possess. Politicians facing growing demands for accountability are looking more carefully at the education budget. Journalists aware of educational reforms in other countries ask more questions about schools in their own. Each is asking harder questions about how schools work and what they produce. The demand for knowledge about educational systems will continue to increase over the next decade or more. It will focus progressively more on questions of quality and outcomes. Most of the data necessary to answer those questions is not systematically collected today. This means that the users of education -- students, parents, and employers -- will increasingly find their questions unanswered. They will be less willing than in the past to accept that absence of information, and more willing to withhold political support for education authorities. It means that political
leaders will decide on education policy without information that might help them make good decisions. Thereby, education may not be as appropriate, as productive or as efficient as it ought to be in a world where good education is increasingly fundamental to economic welfare and social harmony.
III. PRINCIPAL CAUSES
Education is complex
Some portion of these problems owes, of course, to the fact that education is a complex phenomenon. Learning begins at birth, and continues throughout a lifetime. It takes place not only in schools, but also at home, at work, and on the street. And it resides in the mind, which is relatively resistant to direct observation and precise analysis. There is also considerable disagreement over what kinds of learning deserve attention in the context of education, and whether they can be measured. That complexity is reflected in the difficulties countries experience when they record, classify and report education data to UNESCO. Specialists tend to agree that the International Standard Classification of Education (ISCED), developed by UNESCO in 1978, is outdated, culturally bound and sometimes impossible to use. ISCED's concepts and definitions are especially inappropriate for vocational and technical education, but need revision more generally to reflect the major changes that have occurred over the past two decades at several education levels. Classification problems are just one example of how education's complexity makes more difficult efforts to measure the education process. But education's complex character does not mean that we cannot measure and evaluate it. It simply means that we must measure it carefully, in diverse ways and taking into account its multifaceted nature. Most specialists agree that extensive data useful for monitoring and evaluating education can, technically, be developed. They also agree that so far most such data has not been developed. In most countries, the constraints on measurement that education's complexity poses have not been a problem because so little measurement has been attempted.


