Money is not always available
Another explanation for the world's limited progress in monitoring and evaluating education is money. Most countries are poor. Some, particularly in Africa, face extraordinarily severe economic crises. In times of economic hardship, governments are more likely to cut statistics than other government functions. More commonly, governments have been hardpressed to keep up with rapidly expanding enrollments -- exacerbated by the demographic transition - - and have ignored or given low priority to monitoring their educational systems. Funds that might have gone to statistics, analysis and information have gone instead to build new schools and pay teachers' salaries. But lack of money is only one explanation, and in most cases it is probably not the most important. All nations have some money. Virtually all of them decide to spend relatively small amounts on monitoring their educational systems. The fact is that most countries do not assign high priority to statistics and research. They spend the money they have on other items. Better monitoring of educational systems is less a problem of funding than of priorities.
National capacity is often weak or absent
A more fundamental explanation for most countries' failure to properly monitor and evaluate educational systems is the lack of a national capacity to do so. UNESCO's statistics, after all, are only as good as the data governments report. It has no independent ability to gather such data and must, as one internal document notes, "maintain a broad consensus among [governments] as to the amount and type of data that can be requested" (UNESCO, 1992a, 5). National capacities to collect data on educational systems constitute, therefore, a major constraint. Unfortunately, many, and perhaps most, countries lack a professional capacity to gather good education statistics. One good proxy for a sophisticated data collection mechanism is the ability to report data on the ages of pupils/students, which are necessary for calculating net enrollment ratios and school life expectancies (UNESCO, 1993). Only some 60 of UNESCO's 175 member states consistently report such data. Only about half of the remaining countries are able to provide the other data UNESCO requests within reason
able levels of accuracy. The remainder, some 60 countries, find it very difficult to do so. The large developing countries are particularly important in this respect, since they account for such a great proportion of the world's children in school. At least five of the nine largest developing countries encounter serious difficulties in collecting complete and accurate education statistics. Many countries simply cannot now meet UNESCO's global statistical requests (UNESCO staff, personal interviews, 12-13 November 1992). In some countries, these problems stem from a broader inability to govern, brought on by civil strife or economic disaster. But often the capacity problem is more specific: governments have not allocated the funds necessary to establish, equip and staff a proper education statistics office. The people on hand may not have been trained for data collection. They may lack specific expertise in computerizing data. They may change jobs frequently, providing no continuity from one year to the next. They may not have enough money. There may be no effective demand, from the media, policymakers or parents, for better data on education. There may be no incentives to produce it. In some countries education statistics are given less priority than the other statistics that governments routinely collect. Governments often "delegate" the gathering of education statistics to the ministry of education with little funding or supervision. Thus even when governments collect relatively reliable statistics in other areas, they may fall short in education. Often the system for collecting education statistics is totally separate from the country's other statistical systems, and the central bureaucracy makes no effort to develop expertise in the education area. Where capacity is a problem, additional money spent on gathering education statistics may be wasted. Units that lack training, equipment and organization are unlikely to spend properly the resources already available. Thus efforts to improve the quality of data reported by such countries must address first the capacity question. It is difficult for UNESCO, or any other international body, to engage officials in discussions about classification and definitions when those people are more concerned with securing computers or staff. Generating better education statistics, for many countries, depends directly on capacity-building.


