Educational research capacity faces even more serious problems. Its status in developing countries, according to a 1991 review organized jointly by UNESCO's International Institute for Educational Planning (IIEP) and the Institute of International Education (liE) of Stockholm University, "has been uncertain and it has functioned in a milieu of insecurity and isolation" (UNESCO and ILEP 1991b, 1). An assessment currently underway at UNESCO refers to "the poor capacities for, and the paucity of, indigenous evaluation and research in developing countries" (Chinapah, 1992). These deficiencies contrast with the areas of economics and health, where developing countries have made significant advances in establishing research capacity. Capacity to carry out educational research is weak or non-existent in most developing countries. Properly trained people, effective incentives and appropriate institutions are often not in place. The research capacity that does exist tends to be fragile, dominated by a few key individuals, inadequately funded and unable to train its successors. And even when solid research capacity has been established, it often depends for funding almost entirely on fi~reign donors, or is inappropriately controlled by government authorities. In most developing countries it tends to have few effective links to policymakers. As a result, the capacity that is in place nationally produces research that is of mixed quality, and has little impact upon policy.
An assessment mentality is missing
Why is capacity to monitor and evaluate educational systems so weak worldwide? Why do governments fail to assign high priority to such activities? Why do parents, policymakers and the media seldom demand them? Much of the answer derives from the widespread absence of an assessment mentality in education. The idea that education can and should be systematically assessed is not well established around the world. Governments, the general public and even educators may have their doubts, although often for different reasons. Some believe that learning is too elusive to be measured, or if measured, too culturally specific to permit comparisons. For such people, education produces impacts that are so diverse, indirect, unpredictable and even indescribable,
that to measure any one of them is to miss the point of what education is all about. Measurement, from that viewpoint, risks producing assessments that are seriously biased, because so many possible outcomes remain unmeasured. And international comparisons reflect, principally and inappropriately, the values and priorities of the countries that prepare the measuring instrument. Indeed, many who resist measurement in education do so for political as well as epistemological reasons. A significant group sees the quantitative research paradigm as being not only unrealistic, but also imperialistic - - a self-serving construct being promoted by the advanced industrial countries. Although it is difficult to determine how widespread that view is, it is clearly consequential. Another group, larger and more important, simply is not accustomed to measuring many aspects of education, and does not accord it priority. Attempts to improve the quality of data or to devise new kinds of assessment are perceived as a burden or a complication. Here the problem is partly ignorance and partly inertia. Because most aspects of education have for so long gone largely unassessed and unmeasured, it takes time to make the case for a new approach, and to establish new procedures. People must be made aware of the importance of assessment. A culture of measurement needs to be established. This attitude is remarkably widespread, even in countries with substantial experience with educational assessment. OECD, for example, has only recently received comparable education indicators for 1988 from its member countries. It hopes to improve that record gradually, securing 1991 data by the end of 1993. Moreover, it took a year to convince OECD countries that early childhood education was a sufficiently important part of the educational system to warrant including it in the OECD indicators project (OECD staff, personal interview, 12 November 1992). In developing countries the absence, in education, of a culture of measurement is probably the norm. UNESCO specialists talk of encountering "no awareness of, or belief in, the importance of statistics" among some government officials. They describe functionaries who make beautiful declarations regarding data collection, but subsequently carry out no real activity. They report the absence of a "climate" that fosters attention to statistics. The


