mentality, according to one, is that you do not need statistics or research for policy. Another reports that "they don't really believe in it." Often the bureaucracy, standards, and traditions necessary to produce educational assessment do not exist. Not all countries or all government officials elicit such comments. But clearly there is, as yet another specialist put it, a "climate-building" problem (UNESCO staff, personal interviews, 12-13 November 1992). The benefits of educational assessment need to be made clear. Peer pressure needs to be generated. Governments must perceive that this is what good government is about. Because statistics and assessment are not seen to be important, governments often pay little attention to the health and functioning of those units that collect data on the educational system. Thus, for example, investments in training often do not pay off because the trainees are subsequently not employed to do what they were trained to do. As one specialist observed, "You go back, and you find someone else" (UNESCO staff member, personal interview, 13 November 1992). Just as often, the incentives necessary to attract and maintain qualified people are not in place. When appropriate national structures - - salaries, leadership, prestige and demand - - are absent, external inputs, such as training, equipment and technical advice, are unlikely to make a significant difference.
Evaluation is politically unpopular
Yet another explanation for weakness in the worldwide capacity to assess and monitor educational systems is political resistance to being evaluated. This factor is difficult to analyze, because it is rarely expressed. Those motivated by a desire to avoid documenting the shortcomings of their educational systems are unlikely to say so, and may not even realize the source of their resistance. The fear of evaluation, however, is an important obstacle to better educational monitoring and evaluation. Comprehensive, reliable assessments of educational systems may threaten important groups, including politicians, teachers, school administrators, government bureaucrats and policymakers. Evaluation, after all, involves judgment, and may bring change. Measuring efficiency and output may expose some to criticism; others may risk losing their jobs. It may
uncover political patronage, inefficiency, incompetence and low standards. It may generate demands for people to learn new techniques and to work harder. It may energize the media and parents to pay more attention to how schools function. It is unlikely to support the status quo. Good analysis and a solid education database would be revolutionary in most countries. They would change the paradigm of educational management. Most policymakers seek to avoid criticism, and many therefore perceive better education indicators as a threat. Few ministers of education enjoy having the deficiencies of their educational systems demonstrated through solid, empirical data. Few school administrators would want to publicize the fact that many of their students, after spending several years in classrooms, have learned very little. Few politicians are enthusiastic about the prospect of finding their country at the bottom of the list in international comparisons of educational achievement. This attitude is common in all countries, rich or poor. The Ministry of Education in Mexico, for example, is reported to have education outcomes data that it refuses to publish because the results are uncomplimentary (IIEP staff member, personal interview, 12 November 1992). Chile, despite having been a pioneer among developing countries in educational reform, has been reluctant to make public the resuits by school of its annual primary learning assessments. OECD faced serious pressure recently from its member countries to publish the results from its education indicators project (INES) alphabetically, rather than in rank order. Rank-order data revealed that Sweden, for example, was not the best-educated country in Europe, and that the Netherlands was spending relatively large amounts on higher education, and relatively small amounts on compulsory education. That generated, according to one expert, a "tremendous resistance to ranking" among OECD members. Governments were worried about the effects such rankings might have on national policy, and on the images that policymakers had created (OECD staff member, personal interview, 12 November 1992). Moreover, some argue that demonstrating the shortcomings of existing educational systems has no upside potential. "We already know things are bad," goes the argument, "so why evaluate?" The results will be un


INTERNATIONAL EDUCATIONAL STATISTICS AND RESEARCH 89
pleasant, may incur the wrath of the public, and are unlikely to increase prospects for improvement. Resources spent on assessment, according to this position, could be better spent on improving programs that we already know are defective. This attitude makes countries particularly reluctant to compare themselves with others in terms of academic achievement. They see no positive benefit in doing so, and are acutely sensitive to the possibility that they will rank ~t or near the bottom in such comparisons. It is not surprising, for example, that the International Association for the Evaluation of Educational Achievement (lEA) generates relatively little interest among the poorer countries. Policymakers are not keen on the prospect of receiving bad news. Thus they are not generally committed to making educational comparisons based on solid, clear empirical data. And the very idea of cross-national comparisons of educational achievement therefore encounters either apathy or serious resistance. Comparability is not yet a legitimate concept in education. This leads us to a more basic problem: the case for cross-national educational assessments has not been made effectively. Too many countries focus only on the negative aspects of such assessments, and ignore the potential benefits. Some part of this problem is technical. Many countries simply do not understand how cross-national assessments may help them improve their educational systems. There is a need to make that potential clear. But achieving greater acceptance of crossnational educational assessments is not just a technical issue. It is also a question of political legitimacy. At present such assessments are heavily identified with institutions and researchers in the advanced industrial countries. They have little visibility and currency in the developing world. They have not been placed effectively upon the global agenda. Accordingly, governments can easily ignore them. Pressure to participate in cross-national educational assessments comes principally from a few determined researchers or policymakers. Only in a few countries is public opinion beginning to demand more information on educational output, and thus playing a role as well. These efforts, however, are widely scattered, and they lack a global stamp of approval. There is no prominent, international
consensus that such activities should be given serious consideration by all countries. The only two fora that could probably generate that political legitimacy- - UNESCO and ECOSOC - - have so far not chosen to take up the task.
Global leadership is missing
An important part of the problem, therefore, is that a supportive international climate for improving the world's capacity to monitor and evaluate educational systems does not yet exist. Conventional wisdom remains largely unchanged. National officials still do not see why they should invest time and effort in such activities. No comprehensive, high-profile effort to raise consciousness and change behavior around the world is underway. There is no strong international leadership for the cause of better educational assessment. To be sure, several steps are being taken in the right direction. The OECD indicators project, for example, is a pioneering effort to develop education indicators, and marks a significant new direction for the advanced industrial countries that are participating in it. Similarly, the 1990 Jomtien World Declaration on Education for All urges countries to "improve and apply systems of assessing learning achievement" (UNESCO 1990c, 5). That call has prompted the UNICEF/UNESCO Joint Committee on Education to establish a project to develop "national capacities in monitoring basic education goals," which includes inviting a few countries to experiment with school-based sample surveys to measure learning achievement (UNESCO undated, 1). Although this project constitutes, in profile and funding, a minor part of UNESCO's activities, it is nonetheless a promising experiment. The World Bank has promoted the establishment of educational assessment systems in several developing countries, so far with only limited success (Horn et al., 1991). But most of the current initiatives to strengthen educational assessments reside substantially in the advanced industrial countries. IEA and INES have their roots there. The recent initiative to reform ISCED, as a UNESCO document notes, "originated in member states of the OECD and EEC" (UNESCO 1992b, 9). For the rest of the world (the UNESCO/UNICEF initiative notwithstanding), concern for better approaches to educational monitoring and evaluation are, at best, incipient.


