Similarly, the IEA's ongoing difficulties in securing funding for its central operations, and for its projects in developing countries, underscore the absence of international leadership in promoting more and better educational achievement studies. The bulk of IEA's funding comes from European and North American sources, and underwrites studies in those countries. Funding for studies in developing countries is scarce and often precarious. There is little evidence that the pattern of demand is shifting. Even among the advanced industrial countries, it is not clear that IEA's activities are highlyvalued. With the exception of the United States, Canada, the Scandinavians and the Netherlands (and, to a lesser extent, Japan) the advanced industrial countries have provided relatively modest support beyond funding their own country studies. Some governments have argued that they are structurally not able to give money to a non-governmental organization. Others have not understood the costs and value of international achievement studies. Together, they have not taken the initiative to put IEA on a sound financial footing, or to provide funds to expand activities significantly in developing countries. Educational achievement studies therefore remain largely the province of a group of highly committed researchers. No broadly credible international body has taken the lead in making such studies a prominent and regular part of educational assessment worldwide. Another symptom of the tepid interest worldwide in improving educational monitoring and evaluation is the fact that UNESCO has not given such activities special priority. UNESCO is the world's chief source of information on education. Its database is the largest and most comprehensive in existence. But UNESCO has suffered severe economic difficulties over the past decade, and has had to cut back all of its operations. Significantly, it has trimmed resources allocated to its statistical function by roughly the same proportion as resources allocated to other activities. Arguably other parts of UNESCO's program are less essential to conduct through a worldwide intergovernmental organization. But UNESCO has not singled out education statistics, or other aspects of educational monitoring, for special protection from its budget cuts.
As a result, UNESCO's capacity to carry out its statistical function has declined. The organization's statistical unit has shrunk just like the rest of its branches, losing between 35 and 50 per cent of its staff over the past decade. Each regional office used to have a senior statistical officer; today none do. The post of senior professional responsible for statistics on educational finance has been abolished. Recently 34 African countries requested assistance in developing national educational statistics centers. Yet Paris has only two staff members who can provide such assistance, and they can help just six countries each year. Thus although the unit still carries out its core function of statistical tabulation and storage, it has significantly less capacity to undertake such related activities as checking up on the reliability of statistics that governments report, promoting sound statistical practices, publishing partial analyses of its data and providing training in data collection and classification. At a time when demand for information and training from developing countries has been growing, the statistical unit has became a weaker organization. UNESCO's response to these changes has been to treat its statistical function approximately the same as its other functions. The final report of the "Meeting of Experts on Education Indicators and the International Standard Classification of Education (ISCED)" that took place in Paris in June, 1992 urged that UNESCO "mobilize additional extrabudgetary resources for assistance to developing countries in strengthening their national education statistics systems" (UNESCO, 1992b, 12). The organization's capacity to mobilize new resources and to provide the complementary services could depend, however, on the priority it is willing to give to those functions within its current budget. UNESCO's recent initiatives are encouraging, but remain limited. And they have not been matched by a concerted movement among the organization's member states to place education monitoring and evaluation high on its program agenda. Without that response, it will be difficult for UNESCO's Secretariat to move much further. Serious effort from a strong group of member states will be needed if the organization is to reassert leadership over one of its original mandates.


